        <!-- OpenRegister's published register-slug contracts, read as STUBS rather
             than suppressed as unknown classes.
             This repository resolves the two Object* contracts by accident: it keeps
             copies of them under tests/Stubs/Contract/, and psalm reads a symbol from
             that tree even though projectFiles is `lib` only. The register-slug pair
             is deliberately not copied there (tests/bootstrap.php says why), and the
             vendored copies do not help on their own, because `vendor` is under
             ignoreFiles and is never scanned for symbols.
             The obvious fix was a `referencedClass` UndefinedClass suppression, and it
             is the wrong one: suppressing the class makes every value that flows
             through it `mixed`, so psalm stops analysing the very branch this contract
             exists to force. decidesk measured exactly that, and psalm found a real
             NullableReturnStatement within a minute of being able to see the types.
             A stub is read even though vendor is ignored for project files, so the
             types stay live and no suppression is needed.
             Verified in both directions rather than assumed: with these two lines,
             `$resolution->noSuchMethodAtAll()` is reported as UndefinedMethod on
             RegisterSlugResolution, so psalm really is resolving the contract; and the
             six touched files carry no finding of their own beyond the repo-wide
             MissingClassConstType noise. Confirmed a second way: mutating
             ConnectorSourceRegister::slugOrNull() to a non-nullable return - BOTH the
             docblock and the signature, see MismatchingDocblockReturnType below -
             produces two NullableReturnStatement ERRORs and psalm exit 2. So these
             stubs make the analyser bite at this repo's errorLevel="4", they do not
             merely silence it.
             One thing they do NOT buy, measured with @psalm-trace: `isResolved()`
             does not narrow `->slug`. Inside `if ($resolution->isResolved() === true)`
             the property still traces as `null|string`, because the published contract
             carries no @psalm-assert and psalm does not infer one across the call. It
             costs nothing today, since slugOrNull() returns `?string` either way - but
             do not declare a non-nullable return here on the assumption that the
             positive branch narrows. It does not.
             That is fixable at the source rather than per consumer, and is being
             fixed: ConductionNL/openregister#3582 adds
             `@psalm-assert-if-true !null $this->slug` to isResolved(). It has to land
             in openregister's lib/Contract/ AND the hydra-gates copy, which gate 67
             keeps byte identical, then be tagged.
             What that will buy HERE is measured, not assumed. The annotation was
             applied to the vendored contract in a throwaway probe and the trace
             re-run: slugOrNull()'s existing shape narrows `slug` to `string`, and so
             does the same shape with an intervening call, including one that receives
             the whole $resolution. The probe was reverted and both checksums verified.
             `readonly` is NOT what keeps that narrowing, and the difference is the
             point. Probed directly: with `slug` made mutable and an intervening callee
             that sets `$r->slug = null`, psalm still traces `string`, with no error and
             no warning. It is not reasoning about mutability at all. So on a readonly
             property the narrowing is SOUND, because the runtime cannot make it false;
             on a mutable one psalm gives the identical confident green while the value
             really can be null. Anyone copying this pattern to a mutable property gets
             the same clean run and no protection whatsoever.
             The shape is what decides this, and this file is on the right one. Reading
             the property BEFORE the branch does NOT narrow - nothing can narrow a copy
             retroactively - and decidesk uses that shape, which is why its explicit
             null check is load-bearing and this file needs none.
             So when a tag carrying the annotation is installed, slugOrNull() can
             return a non-nullable `string` on the resolved path and this paragraph
             goes. Re-run the trace first: the measurement above was taken against a
             hand-patched vendor file, not a shipped one. -->
        <file name="vendor/conduction/hydra-gates/hydra-gates/contracts/RegisterSlugResolution.php" />
        <file name="vendor/conduction/hydra-gates/hydra-gates/contracts/RegisterSlugResolverInterface.php" />
    </stubs>
